Bundled MCP server (DESIGN.md §7, §12-D7)

Usage: tasqx mcp [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  serve         Run the MCP stdio server: newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0 on stdin/stdout
  instructions  Print the `instructions` string `mcp serve` would send on `initialize` from this directory, then one counts line (D253). Refreshes no docs and changes no data: under --scope write the freshness sweep is a dry run (opening the store migrates it, like any tasqx command), so docs are shown as stored, before that pending refresh

Options:
      --json             Print the raw JSON API result instead of the human table
      --theme <THEME>    Theme name (built-in nord|gruvbox|dracula|solarized|mono, or a user file). Overrides $TASQX_THEME and the config file (DESIGN.md §8)
      --socket <SOCKET>  Local socket / named-pipe address of a tasqx daemon. Overrides $TASQX_SOCK. When a daemon is reachable, one-shot commands route through it (single writer); otherwise they run in-process. A set $TASQX_DB naming a different store than that daemon's is refused. Also selects the address for `daemon` and `watch`
      --no-daemon        Never route through a daemon; always run one-shot in-process (the pre-daemon behaviour). Escape hatch for scripts
  -h, --help             Print help
  -V, --version          Print version

EXAMPLES
  tasqx mcp serve    # serve read-only over stdio JSON-RPC
  tasqx mcp serve --scope write    # serve with explicit write access
  tasqx mcp instructions    # the instructions string initialize sends from here, with its counts

NOTE  Scope configures the local process; it is not authentication. Omitted scope is read-only.
      `instructions` prints exactly what `initialize` would send from the current directory, then one line: rulings, shown, with gist, omitted, and bytes against the cap. `--json` gives instructions, project, inference, shown, gist, omitted, bytes and cap. Under `--scope write` it reports the freshness line from a dry sweep ("would be refreshed"), refreshing no docs and changing no data (opening the store migrates it, like any tasqx command); the docs are shown as stored, before that pending refresh, so a changed imported ruling can differ from a real `initialize`.

See also: api · daemon        Full manual: tasqx manual
